---
id: FRAG-001
type: rule
family: fragile-presentation
title: Content authored invisible pending an entrance animation
version: 1

severity: warning
confidence: medium
spectrum_position: 3

targets: [source]
file_extensions: [.ts, .tsx, .js, .jsx, .mjs, .cjs]
allowlist:
  [
    '**/*.test.ts',
    '**/*.test.tsx',
    '**/*.test.js',
    '**/*.test.jsx',
    '**/*.spec.ts',
    '**/*.spec.tsx',
    '**/*.spec.js',
    '**/*.spec.jsx',
    '**/__tests__/**',
    '**/tests/**',
    '**/explain/**',
  ]

detection:
  type: regex
  pattern: 'initial=\{\{[^}]*\bopacity:\s*0\s*[,}]'

related: []
enabled: true
opt_in: false
---

You're authoring this element invisible (`opacity: 0`) and relying on an
entrance animation to make it visible. If the animation never fires — a
`prefers-reduced-motion` setting, a backgrounded tab that never delivers
the scroll/viewport trigger, a hydration error that strands the initial
style, or JavaScript failing to load — the content stays invisible
permanently. The failure is silent: nothing errors, the section is
simply blank.

Keep content visible by default and animate decoration instead of
existence: enter from a visible state (`initial={{ y: 20 }}` →
`whileInView={{ y: 0 }}`) so a missed trigger costs the motion, not the
content. If a fade-in is deliberate, guarantee the end state — respect
`prefers-reduced-motion` and give a scroll-gated reveal a mount-time
fallback so `opacity: 1` is reached even when the viewport trigger never
fires.

If this element is pure decoration, or a visible reduced-motion/no-JS
fallback provably exists, suppress FRAG-001 with a reason naming the
fallback.
